# Standard HQL library

The declarations HQL ships with live in [`lib/std/`](../../../lib/std/README.md), written
in HQL rather than described in prose, and embedded in the binary. `lib/` holds
the builtin packages, one folder each, and `lib/std/` is the standard package,
one of them: its root holds `prelude.hql`, the package's global space
and the implicit import list every source unit outside the package starts
with, and one folder per module, the module's declarations in its `mod.hql`:
`core`, `doc`, `retrieval`, `graph`, `knowledge`, `collections`, `string`,
`seq`, `math`, `option`, `result`, `regex`, `format`, `io`, `io.net`,
`io.logging`, `vault` and `validators`. No declaration sits outside a module.

Each module is an import unit closed over its own imports
([HQL-0028](../../proposals/HQL-0028/README.md)): the package's prelude does not
apply inside it, so it imports every module whose types or traits it writes,
and binding refuses the environment when a module writes a name it neither
declares nor imports, or imports a module listed after it. Types sit low and
operations above them, so the collection types are `core`'s, since `Data` is a
tree of sequences and maps, and `collections` holds the operations over them.
`string`, `seq`, `math`, `option`, `result` and `regex` are written as external
declarations, `fn trim(self) : String` with no body: HQL declares the contract
and the extension of the same name implements it, which a test holds to the
declaration function by function
([HQL-0024](../../proposals/HQL-0024/README.md), parts 1 and 3).

These cards and those files say the same thing twice, which is a real cost worth
naming. The division is that **the file is the declaration and the card is the
argument**. `lib/std/graph/mod.hql` states that `Link<S, T> : Edge<S, T>`;
[[graph-type]] explains why an edge is the primitive and why its payload is
[[data-type|Data]]. When the two disagree, the card is describing something that
no longer exists, and the card is what gets fixed.

`lib/std/` is deliberately not [the corpus](../../../examples/README.md). The corpus
records what was considered and may keep a withdrawn spelling beside a current
one; a standard library may not. There is one declaration of `Edge`, and it is
the one that is true now.

## Fields and bindings come from the library

A field a program reads on a built-in record — `h.score` on a `Hit`, `c.kind`
on a `Card`, `g.nodes` on a `Graph` — has the type its declaration in `lib/std/`
gives, looked up on the record and then on its parents. The library holds
no binding: `lib/std/vault/mod.hql` declares `Vault`, and `cards = vault.cards` is a
binding of the program module, run before every program over a vault, so
`cards` is an ordinary binding and no special case. A module never sees it
([HQL-0028](../../proposals/HQL-0028/README.md)).

## Declarations are checked, not evaluated

The parser reads the `type` statement, and every file in `lib/std/` checks:
`hql check lib/std/graph/mod.hql` prints `Unit`, and a test checks that every file is
embedded as written and checks over its own imports alone, so a file added there cannot be silently left unchecked.

Checking is not evaluating. A declaration yields `Unit` and introduces no value
and no case in the implemented lattice; constructing a value of a declared
record type is separate work. What holds today is that each declaration is
coherent — its parents exist, its parents can be narrowed together, a parameter
is a name no type already has, and a subtype does not turn a required field
optional — and that where a declaration restates a type the binary already
holds, the two agree. `type Card : Doc` checks; `type Card : Edge` is refused
as a contradiction. The rules are in
[the grammar](../../models/behavior/expressions.md).

That agreement is the point of the folder. `lib/std/` is not a second opinion about
the type lattice; it is the lattice written down where a reader can find it,
and a file that drifts from the binary now fails rather than going unread.

A standard library was a bootstrap non-goal in
[requirements](../../models/requirements/bootstrap.md). It now ships one:
the receiver modules of the prelude, `string` over `String`, `seq` over
`Seq<T>`, `option` and `result`, `math` and `regex`, each a contract in `lib/std/`
with its implementation in the binary.

See [[type-system]] for `:`, generics and refinement, [[hql/core]] for the
vocabulary, and [[hql/extensions]] for the modules the layout follows.
